			  },
			  {
				"type": "formal_statement",
				"title": "Boolean XOR Floor",
				"text": "For primitive Boolean XOR, the minimum-operation construction collapses to a discrete relation.",
				"latex": "d_{\\oplus}(x,y)=\\begin{cases}0,&x=y\\\\1,&x\\neq y\\end{cases}",
				"interpretation": "If \\(x\\neq y\\), then choosing \\(w=x\\oplus y\\) gives \\(x\\oplus w=y\\). Thus one XOR operation is sufficient for every distinct pair. This is an important boundary condition: primitive XOR derives distinction, but does not by itself supply a rich continuous metric."
			  },
			  {
				"type": "prose",
				"text": "The Boolean XOR floor is a boundary condition that must not be overlooked. Primitive XOR supplies a single-step distinction relation because any distinct pair can be related in one XOR operation: \\(x\\oplus(x\\oplus y)=y\\). Consequently, primitive XOR alone does not provide a meaningful multi-step distance or continuous metric geometry. Any such structure requires additional downstream construction, such as multi-step composition, weighted distinctions, or domain-specific interpretation of \\(\\mu_O\\). The foundation is intentionally minimal; richer geometry belongs to later NSFG work."
